diff --git a/docs/MULTI_GPU.md b/docs/MULTI_GPU.md
index ef67fe8d..360fb86f 100644
--- a/docs/MULTI_GPU.md
+++ b/docs/MULTI_GPU.md
@@ -211,6 +211,32 @@ shown: the medians of three 104K reads were 2,803 tok/s before and 2,907 after,
 (its first read faulted 4.2 GB in from NVMe) and there is no A/A restart. Here the page cache absorbed the 10 GB per
 request; with less spare page cache it would be disk traffic. Not measured: both ring variables unset.
 
+**The later cards' K/V whole in VRAM:** `--kv-resident-later N` is `--kv-resident` for the later stages of a split
+(default: the same value). `--kv-resident-later 0` keeps the whole K/V of their attention layers in VRAM, with no
+pinned copy: a second card on a slow link (a chipset x1 slot) then never reads K/V over it during decode, and the
+pinned RAM its copy took is free. The first card streams as before. Under `--prefill-main` the hand-off of a fresh
+prompt's K/V to that card then crosses the link once (about 3 KB per token for three attention layers). Measured on
+an RTX 5090 + RTX 4070 Ti SUPER (x1) with IQ3_S at 262K: decode after 81K-203K prompts +6-9%, 200K prompts
+-5-8%, short prompts within noise; costs the later card about 0.7 GB of VRAM at 262K with 8-bit KV.
+
+**Prompt parts split-wise under `--prefill-main`:** `--prefill-main-small S` (0 = off, the default) gives the later
+stage its own S-token prompt path (own buffers, no loan: its experts stay resident) and reads every text part of a
+prompt of at most `--prefill-main-small-max` tokens (default 8192; set it to the context to chain everything)
+split-wise: the first card runs its own layers out of its expert cache in its usual chunks, hands each chunk whole
+to the stage through one pinned host buffer (a chunk's rows, 40 KB a token: 640 MiB at a 16K chunk), and the stage
+runs its layers from its resident experts in S-token pieces while the first card reads the next chunk. A part is
+cut into about four chunks (at least 1024 tokens, at most the configured chunk) so the two cards overlap and the
+serial tail stays short. A part with image rows is read whole-model on the first card as before, which streams
+the stage's layers' experts from the file for every chunk (about 10.7 GB per 16K chunk on a 9-layer stage with
+IQ3_S). S must be a multiple of 256; at 512 the stage's path stays below the streaming threshold and takes about
+650 MiB on the later card (held back from its expert room, priced by `bytes_needed_owned`; the draft head's
+213 MiB is held back there too now). No conversation checkpoint is taken inside a chained part (the first card's
+session holds only its own layers' state for those cells, and runs a chunk ahead of the stage); the part's end
+checkpoint syncs the stage's state first, like a window read. Measured on the 5090 + 4070 Ti SUPER (x1), IQ3_S:
+fresh prompts of 4K / 8K / 13K / 16K / 32K / 64K read in 2.4 / 2.9 / 4.0 / 5.0 / 8.6 / 15.9 s (whole-model: 2.1 /
+3.5 / 13.4 / 6.4 / 10.6 / 19.0 s with 0.4 / 0.5 / 12 / 11 / 23 / 46 GB from the SSD), 81K in 18.5-20.0 s (24-25 s)
+and 203K in 41 s (57.7 s) with 4.6 / 11.5 GB instead of 57 / 139 GB from the SSD; agent steps of 1.5K / 2.4K /
+5.4K fresh tokens 1.1 / 1.2 / 1.8 s (3.3 / 6.5 / 3.9 s); decode unchanged.
 **A separate VRAM reserve for the later cards:** `--vram-reserve-later-mib N` (default: `--vram-reserve-mib`'s value).
 The card that drives the monitors needs more headroom than one that drives none; with the display on the last card,
 `--vram-reserve-mib 300 --vram-reserve-later-mib 1800` gives the first card's cache that VRAM.
